# Referee's reference sketch (working notes, not a delivery)

Purpose: a minimal independent derivation to check the delivered theory against.
It is not a claim of the study and is not cited by it.

Linear JEPA: context view x_c, target view x_t (masks/augmentations of x), online
encoder W (k x d), predictor P (k x k), target encoder Wbar (EMA of W, stop-grad).
L = 1/2 E||P W x_c - Wbar x_t||^2 + (lam/2)||W||^2 + (lam_P/2)||P||^2.
S_cc = E[x_c x_c^T], S_tc = E[x_t x_c^T].

Gradient flow:
  dW/dt = P^T Wbar S_tc - P^T P W S_cc - lam W
  dP/dt = (Wbar S_tc - P W S_cc) W^T - lam_P P            (O(W^2) near W = 0)
  dWbar/dt = beta (W - Wbar)                               (EMA, beta ~ (1-tau)/step)

Near collapse (W small): the W equation is linear in W, P moves only through its
own decay -lam_P P (the data term is O(||W||^2)). With P ~ p I and commuting
S_cc, S_tc sharing eigenvectors u_i (eigenvalues c_i, t_i), a mode growing at rate
r has Wbar = beta/(beta+r) W, so
  r_i = p t_i * beta/(beta + r_i)  -  p^2 c_i  -  lam     (driving vs decay)
Survival iff p t_i > p^2 c_i + lam at the relevant time (EMA lag only lowers the
driving of fast modes); rank ~ #{i : p t_i - p^2 c_i > lam}.
Things a correct theory of this kind should get right / that I will check:
- the predictor scale enters both sides (driving ~ p, self-prediction decay ~ p^2),
  so there is an optimal predictor scale and too large or too small kills modes;
- predictor weight decay / LR make p time-dependent, a race against W growth;
- masking changes t_i (cross-view covariance) relative to c_i (context
  auto-covariance): with disjoint random masks the diagonal (local/noise) part of
  the covariance drops out of S_tc but not of S_cc;
- EMA momentum enters as the lag factor beta/(beta+r), not as a sign change;
- non-commuting S_cc, S_tc and non-scalar P need the general linear operator, not
  per-eigenvalue scalars; claims stated per "direction" must say which basis.
Prior analyses of the same kind to check novelty against: Tian, Chen & Ganguli
2021 (eigenmode ODEs with predictor, EMA and weight decay); Ziyin et al. 2023
(stability of the collapsed point as a phase transition); Simon et al. 2023
(stepwise eigenmode learning from small init); Littwin et al. 2024 (deep linear
JEPA implicit bias towards high-regression-coefficient features).
